// Build profile: TARGET HAS BLOCKING PRIMITIVES.
//
// One of two mutually-exclusive files declaring wb_dma_cfg_pkg. Exactly one is
// selected into the build; `src/pss/flow.yaml` does the selecting with
// `pss.FileSet`'s `exclude:`, and including both is an error (duplicate
// static const).
//
// This is the ONLY knob in the device tree, and it answers one question: can
// the target runtime SUSPEND A THREAD? That is narrower than "does the target
// have sync_pkg". Both profiles need channel_c, because the in-progress guard
// on wb_dma_ch_c uses try_put()/try_get(), which never block; only the `wake`
// channel's get() does. A C runtime with no scheduler can implement the
// non-blocking subset as a saturating counter and satisfy this profile's
// negation.
//
// What the flag selects, in full:
//
//   true   wb_dma_ch_c.wake, wait_completion(), the three blocking end-to-end
//          operations (transfer_single, transfer_list, stop_channel), their
//          scenario actions, and wb_dma_c::notify_irq().
//   false  none of the above exists. The tree is the non-blocking core only:
//          configuration operations, the three *_start() functions and
//          check_completion(). There is no end-to-end operation, by design --
//          firmware writes its own poll loop, in its own environment, where a
//          spin is free.
//
// Everything the non-blocking profile contains is also executed by this one:
// the blocking wrappers are start() + wait, and wait is a loop over the same
// CSR decode check_completion() uses. So a SystemVerilog regression exercises
// the firmware path on every run without building a second image.
package wb_dma_cfg_pkg {

    static const bool WB_DMA_HAS_BLOCKING = true;

}
